From: Wine Fox
Subject: [PATCH] dcomp, dxgi, win32u, server, winex11, winewayland: Chromium's composition swapchains and cursor (WebView2, the new Epic Games Launcher).

* dcomp, dxgi: Composition swapchains shown in a window, for Chromium's software output.

Programs on Microsoft Edge WebView2 157 (the new Epic Games Launcher, Tauri and others) showed a
white window: WebView2's GPU process died every few seconds on a CHECK and the browser gave up.
Chromium's software compositor (the path it takes with no usable GPU, and the one it falls back
to) draws through SoftwareOutputDeviceWinSwapChain: a WARP D3D11 device, DCompositionCreateDevice,
a target for its own child window, one visual whose content is a swapchain from
CreateSwapChainForComposition, then per frame a copy of the damaged part into buffer 0 and
Present1. Any failure but E_ACCESSDENIED there is a CHECK, and Wine's DCompositionCreateDevice
and CreateSwapChainForComposition returned E_NOTIMPL.

- dcomp: DCompositionCreateDevice returns a minimal IDCompositionDevice: CreateTargetForHwnd,
  CreateVisual, SetRoot, SetContent and Commit work; a target shows the swapchain that is the
  content of its root visual. Child visuals, offsets, transforms and clips are accepted and
  ignored, surfaces, transforms, effects and animations are E_NOTIMPL.
- dxgi: CreateSwapChainForComposition makes a windowed swapchain on the factory's hidden device
  window; dcomp's Commit tells it (a private GUID with SetPrivateData) which window it is shown
  in. Present of such a swapchain copies buffer 0 to that window with GDI: Chromium's window is a
  child of the browser's, in another process, which GL presentation does not reach, while GDI
  does under X11. Buffer 0 is never rotated, so Chromium's damage-only updates keep a full image.

Under Wayland a window of another process' top level still shows nothing (winewayland draws
only in the windows of its own process): run such programs with X11.

* win32u, dxgi: Composition frames drawn by the process of the top level, for Wayland.

With 0029 programs on WebView2 157 (the new Epic Games Launcher) showed their window under X11
only: under winewayland it stayed white. Chromium's GPU process draws in its own "Intermediate
D3D Window", a child of the browser's window, itself a child of the program's top level, and
winewayland draws only in the windows of the process that owns the top level: a DC of that
window in the GPU process has no surface, and GL presentation does not reach it either.

- win32u: a new internal message, WM_WINE_COMPOSITION_PRESENT, sent to the top level: wparam is
  a window under it, lparam a section handle in the receiving process with the frame (a small
  header and 32-bit rows). The process of the top level draws it in that window with GDI, into
  its own window surface, and closes the handle. The frame and the window are checked first.
- dxgi: Present of a composition swapchain whose window's top level belongs to another process
  and is not an X11 window copies buffer 0 into a section, duplicates its handle into that
  process and sends it the message (100 ms timeout). Under X11 GDI still draws from the GPU
  process directly, as with 0029.

* dxgi: Swapchains in a window of another process' top level presented as composition ones.

With 0029 and 0030 the new Epic Games Launcher (WebView2 157) showed its UI where Chromium
composites in software (no usable GPU), but on a real GPU (AMD, Mesa) its window stayed a solid
fill under X11 and Wayland. With GPU compositing Chromium's GPU process presents a regular
windowed D3D11 swapchain (ANGLE) in its window, a child of the browser's window, whose top level
is the program's, in other processes. GL presentation does not show there: winewayland draws
only in the top levels of its own process, and under X11 the top level's process paints over it.

Present of a windowed D3D11 swapchain whose window's top level belongs to another process now
goes the way of a composition swapchain (0029, 0030): buffer 0 is read back and drawn with GDI,
from here under X11 and by the top level's process under Wayland. Buffer 0 is not rotated, as for
composition swapchains. R8G8B8A8 back buffers are accepted too (swapped to BGRA for GDI).
Shown with a test of two processes (a hardware D3D11 swapchain in a child of the other process'
window), under winex11 and winewayland.

* server, winex11, winewayland: The cursor over a child of another process' top level.

The new Epic Games Launcher (WebView2) showed no cursor over its window under Wayland. Its content
is a child window of Chromium's browser process in the launcher's top level. That process gets
the mouse messages there and sets the cursor, but the server took the top level as the cursor
window, so the cursor the browser process set went nowhere, and WM_WINE_SETCURSOR brought the
top level's process its own cursor, none.

The server now takes the window under the pointer as the cursor window when it belongs to
another process than its top level: its thread is the one that gets the mouse messages
(window_thread_from_point). The driver of that process then sets the cursor for a window of a
top level it does not own: winex11 on the top level's X window, which is the display's;
winewayland, where only the top level's process has the pointer, posts the cursor's shape
(cursor-shape-v1) to the top level, whose process sets it while the pointer is over it.
Windows of one process are as before.
Shown with a test of two processes under winex11 (WM_WINE_SETCURSOR reaches the child's process).

